        "content": "#include <stdio.h>\\n#include <stdlib.h>\\n#include <string.h>\\n#include <math.h>\\n\\n#define N_ITER 10 // number of simulation iterations\\n#define DT 0.01f // time step\\n#define SOFTENING 1e-9f // to avoid zero divisors\\n\\n/*\\n * Each body holds coordinate positions (i.e., x, y, and z) and\\n * velocities (i.e., vx, vy, and vz).\\n */\\ntypedef struct { float x, y, z, vx, vy, vz; } Body;\\n\\n/*\\n * Compute the gravitational impact on one target body.\\n */\\nstatic void body_force_one(Body *p, float dt, int n, int i) {\\n  float fx = 0.0f;\\n  float fy = 0.0f;\\n  float fz = 0.0f;\\n\\n  for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {\\n    float dx = p[j].x - p[i].x;\\n    float dy = p[j].y - p[i].y;\\n    float dz = p[j].z - p[i].z;\\n    float sqrd_dist = dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz + SOFTENING;\\n    float inv_dist = 1 / sqrt(sqrd_dist);\\n    float inv_dist3 = inv_dist * inv_dist * inv_dist;\\n\\n    fx += dx * inv_dist3;\\n    fy += dy * inv_dist3;\\n    fz += dz * inv_dist3;\\n  }\\n\\n  p[i].vx += dt*fx;\\n  p[i].vy += dt*fy;\\n  p[i].vz += dt*fz;\\n}\\n\\n/*\\n * Read a binary dataset with initilized bodies.\\n */\\nBody* read_dataset(int *nbodies) {\\n  int b = fread(nbodies, sizeof(*nbodies), 1, stdin);\\n  if (b != 1) {\\n    fprintf(stderr,\"\\\\nError reading nbodie value\\\\n\");\\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\\n  }\\n  Body *bodies = (Body *)malloc(*nbodies * sizeof(Body));\\n  b = fread(bodies, *nbodies * sizeof(Body), 1, stdin);\\n  if (b != 1) {\\n    fprintf(stderr,\"\\\\nError reading input values\\\\n\");\\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\\n  }\\n\\n  return bodies;\\n}\\n\\n/*\\n * Write simulation results into a binary dataset.\\n */\\nvoid write_dataset(const int nbodies, Body *bodies) {\\n  int b = fwrite(bodies, nbodies * sizeof(Body), 1, stdout);\\n  if (b != 1) {\\n    fprintf(stderr,\"\\\\nError writing to output\\\\n\");\\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nint main(int argc,char **argv) {\\n  int nbodies;\\n\\n  Body *bodies = read_dataset(&nbodies);\\n\\n  /*\\n   * Keep one team for all iterations. The static target assignment has\\n   * uniform work, and the implicit barriers preserve the force/integration\\n   * phase ordering.\\n   */\\n  #pragma omp parallel if (nbodies >= 64)\\n  {\\n    for (int iter = 0; iter < N_ITER; iter++) {\\n      #pragma omp for schedule(static)\\n      for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; i++) {\\n        body_force_one(bodies, DT, nbodies, i);\\n      }\\n\\n      #pragma omp for schedule(static)\\n      for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; i++) {\\n        bodies[i].x += bodies[i].vx * DT;\\n        bodies[i].y += bodies[i].vy * DT;\\n        bodies[i].z += bodies[i].vz * DT;\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  write_dataset(nbodies, bodies);\\n\\n  free(bodies);\\n  \\n  exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);\\n}\\n",
        "path": "src/nbody.c"
      }
    ],
    "hypothesis": "A persistent OpenMP team with static worksharing should divide the uniform per-target O(n²) force work across the four cores while amortizing team startup. Each target retains its original ascending source-body order and independent accumulators, preserving floating-point accumulation order and output correctness.",
    "notes": "The parallel region is enabled only at 64 or more bodies to avoid team overhead for smaller inputs. Implicit worksharing barriers keep force calculation and position integration ordered. No execution or correctness testing was performed.",
